# ManageMeStock

ManageMeStock is the Salvatori Butchers desktop administration application.

The application is built with Python and PyQt5 and connects to services including:

* Supabase / PostgreSQL
* Sage
* Internal Salvatori services
* Excel/reporting tools
* Dibal scale integrations

The project supports separate **development** and **production** environments so changes can be developed without modifying live Salvatori data.

---

# Environments

ManageMeStock currently uses:

```text
development
production
```

The active application environment is controlled by:

```text
APP_ENV
```

The Git branch and Python virtual environment do **not** determine which application environment is being used.

For example, being on:

```text
development_environment
```

does not automatically put the application into development mode.

Likewise, activating:

```text
.venv
```

only changes the Python installation and dependencies being used.

The application environment itself is controlled by `APP_ENV`.

---

# Development Environment

The preferred way to run development is:

```bash
./manage.sh dev
```

This starts ManageMeStock with:

```text
APP_ENV=development
```

Development currently uses:

* Python `.venv`
* Local Supabase through Docker
* Local PostgreSQL database
* Local copy of production Supabase data
* Dummy Sage products
* Dummy Sage invoices
* Dummy Sage invoice items
* Dummy Sage customer structure
* No live Sage API requests

When development starts correctly, the terminal should show:

```text
ManageMeStock environment: development
```

---

# Production Environment

Production uses:

* Production Supabase
* Production PostgreSQL
* Real Sage API
* Real Salvatori data and services

Preferred command:

```bash
./manage.sh prod
```

The application can also be started directly with:

```bash
python3 main.py
```

If `APP_ENV` has not been explicitly set, ManageMeStock defaults to:

```text
production
```

The terminal should therefore show:

```text
ManageMeStock environment: production
```

when running against live services.

Always check the startup environment before performing operations that may modify data.

---

# Python Virtual Environment

The development Python environment is stored in:

```text
.venv/
```

Activate it manually with:

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
```

The terminal should then show:

```text
(.venv)
```

Deactivate it with:

```bash
deactivate
```

Install project dependencies with:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

---

# Running Development Manually

Normally use:

```bash
./manage.sh dev
```

If required, development can also be launched manually with:

```bash
APP_ENV=development .venv/bin/python3 main.py
```

This explicitly starts the application using:

```text
APP_ENV=development
```

---

# manage.sh Commands

Start development:

```bash
./manage.sh dev
```

Start production:

```bash
./manage.sh prod
```

Check local Supabase status:

```bash
./manage.sh status
```

Reset the local development database:

```bash
./manage.sh reset-db
```

Stop local Supabase:

```bash
./manage.sh stop
```

---

# Local Supabase

Development uses a local Supabase instance running through Docker.

Docker Desktop must be available.

Start Docker manually if required:

```bash
open -a Docker
```

Check Docker:

```bash
docker info
```

Start local Supabase:

```bash
supabase start
```

Check its status:

```bash
supabase status
```

Local Supabase Studio is normally available at:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:54323
```

The local Supabase API is normally:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:54321
```

The local PostgreSQL database is normally:

```text
127.0.0.1:54322
```

---

# Resetting the Development Database

Reset the local database using:

```bash
supabase db reset
```

or:

```bash
./manage.sh reset-db
```

This recreates the local database using the Supabase migrations and local seed data.

Do **not** run:

```bash
supabase db reset --linked
```

for normal development work.

The linked Supabase project is the live hosted project.

---

# Supabase Schema

The Supabase schema is stored as migrations under:

```text
supabase/migrations/
```

To pull the latest production schema:

```bash
supabase db pull
```

If the production schema has changed, refresh development with:

```bash
supabase db pull
supabase db dump --data-only --linked > supabase/seed.sql
supabase db reset
```

---

# Refreshing Local Production Data

Development currently uses a local copy of production database data.

If only production data has changed:

```bash
supabase db dump --data-only --linked > supabase/seed.sql
supabase db reset
```

If both the production schema and data have changed:

```bash
supabase db pull
supabase db dump --data-only --linked > supabase/seed.sql
supabase db reset
```

This updates the local development environment without requiring the application to work directly against the live database.

---

# Important: seed.sql

The file:

```text
supabase/seed.sql
```

contains a copy of real Salvatori production data.

It must **never be committed to GitHub**.

It should be ignored through:

```text
supabase/.gitignore
```

---

# Sage Development Setup

Production uses the real Sage API.

Development uses local dummy Sage data instead.

This allows Sage-related functionality to be developed without sending requests to the live Salvatori Sage system.

Shared Sage connection logic is stored in:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/sage_connection.py
```

This contains common Sage functionality such as:

* checking whether dummy Sage should be used
* internal network detection
* Sage API URL selection
* Sage API token loading
* Sage configuration validation

This prevents the same connection code being duplicated across each Sage controller.

---

# Sage Controllers

Sage controllers are stored under:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/
```

Current controllers include:

```text
products.py
invoices.py
invoice_items.py
customers.py
```

Individual controllers should contain the functionality for their specific Sage resource.

Shared connection and environment behaviour should remain inside:

```text
sage_connection.py
```

rather than being duplicated between controllers.

---

# Dummy Sage Data

Dummy Sage data is stored under:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/dummy_data/
```

The current structure is:

```text
controllers/
└── sage_controllers/
    ├── sage_connection.py
    ├── products.py
    ├── invoices.py
    ├── invoice_items.py
    ├── customers.py
    └── dummy_data/
        ├── __init__.py
        ├── products.py
        ├── invoices.py
        ├── invoice_items.py
        └── customers.py
```

---

# Dummy Sage Products

Dummy products are stored in:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/dummy_data/products.py
```

The current dummy product structure includes fields such as:

```text
STOCK_CODE
DESCRIPTION
```

The product controller uses this data instead of calling the live Sage API while in development.

---

# Dummy Sage Invoices

Dummy invoices are stored in:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/dummy_data/invoices.py
```

The current dummy invoice data contains fields required by the existing Sage invoice functionality, including:

```text
INVOICE_NUMBER
ACCOUNT_REF
INVOICE_DATE
RECORD_CREATE_DATE
```

The dummy invoice structure mirrors the existing Sage response format:

```python
{
    "results": [
        ...
    ]
}
```

This is important because existing application code expects invoice responses to contain a `results` collection.

---

# Dummy Sage Invoice Items

Dummy invoice items are stored in:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/dummy_data/invoice_items.py
```

The current structure includes fields such as:

```text
INVOICE_NUMBER
STOCK_CODE
QUANTITY
RECORD_CREATE_DATE
```

Invoice numbers and stock codes correspond with the dummy invoice and product datasets so the development data remains consistent.

---

# Dummy Sage Customers

Dummy customer data is stored in:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/dummy_data/customers.py
```

There is currently no Sage customer-fetching functionality in the application.

The customer dummy-data file therefore currently exists as scaffolding and can be expanded once customer functionality is added.

---

# Sage Development Behaviour

When ManageMeStock is running with:

```text
APP_ENV=development
```

the Sage controllers use their dummy datasets.

They do not retrieve the real Sage API configuration or make requests to the production Sage service.

When ManageMeStock is running with:

```text
APP_ENV=production
```

the controllers use the real Sage configuration through:

```text
controllers/sage_controllers/sage_connection.py
```

and continue using the existing live Sage API functionality.

---

# Environment Configuration

Development configuration is stored in:

```text
.env.development
```

Production configuration is stored in:

```text
.env
```

Environment files containing credentials should not be committed to GitHub.

Important files that should remain ignored include:

```text
.env
.env.development
.venv/
supabase/seed.sql
```

---

# Files That Should Be Committed

The following development-environment files should normally be committed:

```text
requirements.txt
requirements-dev.txt
manage.sh
supabase/config.toml
supabase/migrations/
supabase/.gitignore
controllers/sage_controllers/
README.md
```

---

# Files That Should Not Be Committed

Do not commit:

```text
.venv/
.env
.env.development
supabase/seed.sql
```

Always check before committing:

```bash
git status
```

---

# Recommended Development Workflow

Start the development environment:

```bash
./manage.sh dev
```

Confirm:

```text
ManageMeStock environment: development
```

Make development changes.

Check files before committing:

```bash
git status
```

Stage changes:

```bash
git add .
```

Commit:

```bash
git commit -m "Description of changes"
```

Push the development branch:

```bash
git push
```

---

# Safety Rule

Development work must not modify live Salvatori production data.

Before using the application for development, confirm the terminal shows:

```text
ManageMeStock environment: development
```

If the application unexpectedly reports:

```text
ManageMeStock environment: production
```

stop before performing development actions.

Development should use:

```text
Local Supabase
Dummy Sage
```

while production uses:

```text
Production Supabase
Real Sage
```
